Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  therap: ["希腊语 therapeuein(侍奉、照料、治疗)来自 theraps(侍从);词源不明。荷马的 therapōn 是英雄的侍从(帕特罗克洛斯是阿喀琉斯的 therapōn);therapeia 先是侍奉神明,后指照料病人——希波克拉底的医疗;therapeutikos 治疗的(therapeutic 1640 年代),therapy 1846(先指医学治疗),psycho-therapy 1853,physio-therapy 1905,chemo-therapy 1907(埃尔利希),therapist 1880 年代,Therapeutae 是斐洛记载的埃及犹太隐修派。",
    "Greek therapeuein (to attend, tend, heal) comes from theraps (an attendant); its origin is unknown. Homer's therapōn is a hero's squire (Patroclus is Achilles' therapōn); therapeia was first the service of the gods, then the tending of the sick — Hippocratic treatment; therapeutikos healing (therapeutic 1640s), therapy 1846 (medical at first), psycho-therapy 1853, physio-therapy 1905, chemo-therapy 1907 (Ehrlich), therapist 1880s, the Therapeutae Philo's Jewish contemplatives of Egypt."],
  bat: ["battuere(打)是拉丁语从高卢语借的俗语词(与高卢语 *battu- 同源),古典拉丁语几乎不用,却成了罗曼语的\"打\":法语 battre、意大利语 battere、西班牙语 batir。battalia 是打仗——battle 经法语 bataille,battalion 意大利语 battaglione(大战队,1580 年代),com-battere 一起打(combat),de-battere 打散——辩论(debate 是法语 débattre),a-battre 打倒(abattoir 屠宰场 1820 年代),re-battere 打回——rebate 折扣、rebut 反驳,batter 面糊是\"打\"出来的,battery 先是殴打,再是炮组(一起\"打\"的炮),1749 年富兰克林用于电池组,bat 球棒可能同源,baton 指挥棒,battuta 意大利语拍子。",
    "battuere (to beat) was a colloquial Latin word borrowed from Gaulish (*battu-), rare in classical texts but the Romance word for hitting: French battre, Italian battere, Spanish batir. battalia was fighting — battle through French bataille, battalion Italian battaglione, a big fighting band (1580s), com-battere to fight together (combat), de-battere to beat down — to debate (French débattre), a-battre to knock down (abattoir 1820s), re-battere to beat back — rebate, rebut, batter the beaten mixture, battery first an assault, then a group of guns firing together, then Franklin's 1749 set of Leyden jars, bat the club possibly the same root, baton, battuta the Italian beat."],
  sed: ["sedere(坐)来自印欧语 *sed-(坐),同根有希腊语 hezomai、hedra(座——cathedral 是主教座堂,polyhedron 多面体)、梵语 sad、英语 sit、seat、settle、德语 sitzen、俄语 sidet'。罗马人 sedere 坐,sedes 座位、住所(see 主教的教区是它经法语 sied),sessio 会议(session),sedentarius 久坐的,prae-sidere 坐在前面主持(preside、president),re-sidere 留住(reside、residue 剩下的),sub-sidium 后备队坐在后面——补助(subsidy),ob-sidere 坐在城前——围攻(obsession 是被\"围坐\"的心),as-siduus 一直坐着——勤勉(assiduous),in-sidiae 坐着等——埋伏(insidious),dis-sidere 坐得远——异议(dissident),siege 是 sedicum 经法语,nest 是 ni-sd-os\"坐下去的地方\"。",
    "sedere (to sit) comes from PIE *sed- (to sit), shared with Greek hezomai, hedra (a seat — a cathedral holds the bishop's chair, a polyhedron has many faces), Sanskrit sad, English sit, seat, settle, German sitzen, Russian sidet'. Roman sedes a seat, a dwelling (a bishop's see is sedes through French sied), sessio a sitting (session), sedentarius sitting much, prae-sidere to sit in front (preside, president), re-sidere to stay seated (reside; residue what stays), sub-sidium the reserve sitting behind — support (subsidy), ob-sidere to sit before a city — besiege (an obsession besieges the mind), as-siduus always sitting — assiduous, in-sidiae a sitting in wait — ambush (insidious), dis-sidere to sit apart (dissident), siege sedicum through French, nest ni-sd-os, the sitting-down place."],
  bon: ["bonus(好)是古拉丁语 duenos(前 6 世纪的\"Duenos 铭文\")的后代,来自印欧语 *dw-eno-(好),与 bene(好地)、beatus(幸福)同根;英语没有亲戚。罗马人的 bonum 是善,summum bonum 至善;bene-dicere 说好话(benediction),bene-ficium 恩惠(benefit),bene-volens 好意(benevolent),bene-factor 行善者,bonitas 善良(bounty 是它经法语 bonté),bonus 在 18 世纪伦敦交易所成了\"额外红利\"(拉丁语用错了性,应是 bonum),bon 法语好(bon voyage、bonbon 是儿语\"好好\"),buono 意大利语,bueno 西班牙语,boon(恩惠)是另一个古挪威词但受它影响,debonair 出身好的。",
    "bonus (good) descends from Old Latin duenos (the 6th-century BC Duenos inscription), PIE *dw-eno- (good), kin to bene (well) and beatus (blessed); English has no cognate. Roman bonum is the good, summum bonum the highest good; bene-dicere to speak well (benediction), bene-ficium a kindness (benefit), bene-volens well-wishing (benevolent), bene-factor a doer of good, bonitas goodness (bounty through French bonté), bonus became an extra dividend on the 18th-century London exchange (in the wrong gender — it should be bonum), French bon (bon voyage; bonbon is baby-talk \"good-good\"), Italian buono, Spanish bueno, debonair of good stock; boon is Old Norse, coloured by bonus."],
  alt: ["altus(高、深)是 alere(养育)的古分词——\"长高了的\";印欧语 *h₂el-(养),同根有英语 old(\"长成了的\")、elder、adult(ad-ultus 长成的)、德语 alt。罗马人 altus 既指高也指深(alta mare 深海);altitudo 高度(altitude),ex-altare 举高(exalt),altare 祭坛(高台),alto 意大利语高——中音(比女高音低的\"高\"声部),haughty 是 altus 经法语 haut(h 是日耳曼语 hoch 的影响),enhance 是 in-altiare 抬高,altimeter 1918,contralto,alt- 的兄弟 alumnus(被养的——校友)、alimony(赡养费)、proletarius(只有后代 proles 的人)。",
    "altus (high, deep) is the old participle of alere (to nourish) — \"grown up\"; PIE *h₂el- (to nourish), shared with English old (fully grown), elder, adult (ad-ultus, grown), German alt. Roman altus was both high and deep (alta mare, the deep sea); altitudo height (altitude), ex-altare to raise up (exalt), altare the high altar, alto Italian high — the voice \"high\" below the soprano, haughty altus through French haut (the h from Germanic hoch), enhance in-altiare, to raise, altimeter 1918, contralto; its siblings from alere are alumnus (the nourished — a former pupil), alimony (maintenance), proletarius (a man with nothing but offspring, proles)."],
  cas: ["cadere(落)来自印欧语 *ḱad-(落),同根有梵语 śad-(落)、威尔士语 codwm?存疑。罗马人 cadere 说树叶落、人倒下、事情发生(accidere 落到身上);casus 是落、也是\"情况\"(case;语法的格 casus 是斯多葛派的\"落\"——从主格落下的形式),casualis 偶然的(casual;casualty 意外伤亡),oc-casus 日落——西方(occident),oc-casio 落到面前的机会(occasion),cadaver 倒下的尸体,de-cadentia 衰落(decadence 1540 年代),cascade 意大利语 cascata 瀑布,chance 是 cadentia 经法语——落下的骰子,cadence 节奏的\"落\",coincide 一起落下,deciduous 会落叶的,accident 落到身上的事,recidivist 再次跌倒的人。",
    "cadere (to fall) comes from PIE *ḱad- (to fall), shared with Sanskrit śad- (to fall). Romans cadere of leaves, of the fallen, of what befalls (accidere); casus a fall and a circumstance (case; the grammatical case is the Stoics' \"falling\" from the nominative), casualis by chance (casual; a casualty), oc-casus the sun's setting — the west (occident), oc-casio what falls in your way (occasion), cadaver the fallen body, de-cadentia a falling away (decadence 1540s), cascade Italian cascata, chance cadentia through French — the fall of the dice, cadence the fall of the voice, coincide to fall together, deciduous leaf-shedding, accident what befalls, recidivist one who falls again."],
  prehend: ["prehendere(抓)是 prae-(前)+ *hendere(抓,与希腊语 khandanein 容纳、英语 get 同根,印欧语 *gʰed-)。罗马人 prehendere 抓住;prehensio 抓——prison 是它经法语 prisun(先指逮捕,后指监狱),prisoner 被抓的人,ap-prehendere 抓住——理解、也指害怕(apprehend、apprehension 两义并存),com-prehendere 全抓住——理解(comprehend;法语 comprendre、意大利语 comprendere、西班牙语 comprender 都是\"懂\"),re-prehendere 抓回来责备(reprehensible),sur-prise 是 super-prehendere 从上面抓住(surprise 经法语 surprendre),enterprise 是 inter-prehendere\"着手\"(法语 entreprendre),prize(战利品)是 prise 抓来的,pregnant?否,apprentice 是 apprendre 学徒——学就是抓。",
    "prehendere (to grasp) is prae- (before) + *hendere (to seize, kin to Greek khandanein, to hold, and English get; PIE *gʰed-). Romans prehendere to seize; prehensio a seizing — prison through French prisun (an arrest before a jail), prisoner the seized, ap-prehendere to grasp — to understand and to fear (apprehend keeps both), com-prehendere to grasp wholly — to comprehend (French comprendre, Italian comprendere, Spanish comprender all mean to understand), re-prehendere to take back and blame (reprehensible), surprise super-prehendere, to seize from above, through French surprendre, enterprise inter-prehendere, to take in hand (French entreprendre), a prize a thing taken, an apprentice one who apprend — learning is grasping."],
  rur: ["rus(乡村,属格 ruris)来自印欧语 *reuh₁-(空地、开阔),同根有英语 room、德语 Raum、阿维斯陀语 ravah(空间)。罗马人 rus 是与 urbs 相对的乡下(O rus, quando ego te aspiciam——贺拉斯);ruralis 乡村的(rural 1400 年代),rusticus 乡下的——粗野的(rustic 1440 年代),rusticitas 土气,rustle 与此无关,rusticate 是牛津剑桥\"下乡\"处分(停学),roomy 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "rus (the country, genitive ruris) comes from PIE *reuh₁- (open space), shared with English room, German Raum, Avestan ravah (space). Roman rus was the country as against the urbs (O rus, quando ego te aspiciam — Horace); ruralis rural (1400s), rusticus of the country — unpolished (rustic 1440s), rusticitas rusticity, rusticate the Oxbridge sentence of sending a student down to the country; room is its Germanic cousin."],
  eur: ["拉丁语的施事后缀 -tor(阴性 -trix)加在分词词干上:actor 做的人、victor 胜者、imperator 统帅、scriptor 作者。它在法语里磨成 -eur(阴性 -euse/-trice:acteur、actrice、vendeur/vendeuse),西班牙语 -dor/-dora(vendedor、trabajador),意大利语 -tore/-trice(attore、attrice、pittore),英语 -or(actor、doctor)与 -er 竞争(日耳曼语 -er 也是 -arius 借来的);-eur 在法语里还造了 chauffeur、amateur、entrepreneur——英语直接借走。工具义(computer、tractor)是同一个后缀:做事的东西。",
    "The Latin agent suffix -tor (feminine -trix) sits on the participial stem: actor the doer, victor the winner, imperator the commander, scriptor the writer. French wore it to -eur (feminine -euse/-trice: acteur, actrice, vendeur/vendeuse), Spanish to -dor/-dora (vendedor, trabajador), Italian to -tore/-trice (attore, attrice, pittore), English keeps -or (actor, doctor) beside Germanic -er (itself borrowed from Latin -arius); French -eur also produced chauffeur, amateur, entrepreneur, which English took whole. The instrument sense (computer, tractor) is the same suffix: the thing that does."],
  té: ["拉丁语抽象名词后缀 -tas(属格 -tatis)加在形容词上:veritas 真、libertas 自由、civitas 公民身份、qualitas 性质(西塞罗为译希腊语 poiotēs 造的)。它经法语变成 -té(vérité、liberté、cité),西班牙语 -dad/-tad(verdad、libertad、ciudad),意大利语 -tà(verità、libertà、città),英语 -ty(经法语 -té:verity、liberty、city)——所以 city、cité、ciudad、città 都是 civitas。英语又加了 -ity(经法语 -ité:quality、reality),与 -ness(日耳曼)并行。",
    "The Latin abstract suffix -tas (genitive -tatis) sits on adjectives: veritas truth, libertas freedom, civitas citizenship, qualitas quality (Cicero's coinage for Greek poiotēs). Through French it became -té (vérité, liberté, cité), Spanish -dad/-tad (verdad, libertad, ciudad), Italian -tà (verità, libertà, città), English -ty via French -té (verity, liberty, city) — so city, cité, ciudad and città are all civitas. English also has -ity (via French -ité: quality, reality) alongside Germanic -ness."],
  ment: ["拉丁语后缀 -mentum 造\"手段、结果\"的名词:instrumentum 工具、monumentum 纪念物、fragmentum 碎片、documentum 教训。法语把它变成 -ment(mouvement、gouvernement),又用 -ment 造副词(lentement——本是 mente\"以…的心\"的夺格:lenta mente 以慢的心;西班牙语 -mente、意大利语 -mente 同理,所以 -mente 副词是\"用某种心态\"),英语借了名词的 -ment(movement、government、judgment),连日耳曼词也加(merriment、amazement)。",
    "The Latin suffix -mentum makes nouns of means and result: instrumentum a tool, monumentum a memorial, fragmentum a piece, documentum a lesson. French turned it into -ment (mouvement, gouvernement) and separately built adverbs in -ment (lentement — originally the ablative mente, \"with a … mind\": lenta mente, with a slow mind; Spanish -mente and Italian -mente likewise, so a -mente adverb means \"in such a frame of mind\"); English borrowed the noun -ment (movement, government, judgment) and added it to Germanic words too (merriment, amazement)."],
  age: ["拉丁语后缀 -aticum(属于…的、与…有关的中性名词)在法语里磨成 -age:viaticum → voyage,silvaticus → sauvage(野的),formaticum(成型的奶酪)→ fromage,homin-aticum → hommage(向主人的效忠)。它在法语里成了极能产的后缀:courage(心气)、langage、mariage、bagage、fromage;英语从法语借了大批 -age(marriage、village、language、courage、damage),又自己造(postage、mileage、wreckage)。西班牙语对应 -aje(viaje、lenguaje),意大利语 -aggio(viaggio、linguaggio)。",
    "The Latin suffix -aticum (belonging to, connected with) wore down in French to -age: viaticum → voyage, silvaticus → sauvage (wild), formaticum (cheese shaped in a form) → fromage, homin-aticum → hommage (a vassal's loyalty). In French it became hugely productive: courage, langage, mariage, bagage, fromage; English borrowed a crowd of -age words (marriage, village, language, courage, damage) and coined more (postage, mileage, wreckage). Spanish has -aje (viaje, lenguaje), Italian -aggio (viaggio, linguaggio)."],
  aud: ["audire(听)来自印欧语 *h₂ew-is-dʰh₁-(\"把感知放进去\"——注意听),与希腊语 aisthanesthai(感知)同根。罗马人 audire 听;auditor 听者(auditor 审计员是\"听账\"的人——中世纪账目是念给他听的),auditorium 听讲堂,audientia 听讲——觐见、观众(audience),audit 审计 1430 年代,audible 可听见,ob-audire 听从——obey(经法语 obéir),ob-oediens 顺从(obedient),audio 1930 年代作前缀,audition 1590 年代指听觉、1880 年代指试演,auditory 听觉的。",
    "audire (to hear) comes from PIE *h₂ew-is-dʰh₁- (\"to put perception in\" — to give ear), kin to Greek aisthanesthai (to perceive). Roman auditor a hearer (an auditor of accounts once heard them read aloud), auditorium a hall for hearing, audientia a hearing — an audience, audit 1430s, audible, ob-audire to give ear to — obey (through French obéir), ob-oediens compliant (obedient), audio as a prefix 1930s, audition hearing in the 1590s and a trial performance in the 1880s, auditory."],
  cre: ["creare(造、生)与 crescere(生长)来自印欧语 *ḱer-(生长),同根有 Ceres(谷物女神——cereal)、英语 无(create 是借词)、希腊语 koros(少年)、亚美尼亚语 ser(后代)。罗马人 creare 是生育、也是选举产生(consulem creare 选出执政官);creator 造物主(基督教用语),creatura 受造物(creature),re-creare 恢复(recreation 娱乐是\"重新造人\"),pro-creare 生育,crescere 生长——crescent 是\"正在长的\"月亮(法语 croissant 是月牙面包),in-crementum 增长(increment),de-crescere 减少(decrease),con-crescere 长在一起——凝固(concrete),ac-crescere 增长(accrue),crew 是 accrue 的缩写——增补的人手,recruit 是 recroistre 再长出来。",
    "creare (to make, bring forth) and crescere (to grow) come from PIE *ḱer- (to grow), shared with Ceres (the grain goddess — cereal), Greek koros (a youth), Armenian ser (offspring). Roman creare was to beget and to elect (consulem creare, to make a consul); creator the maker (a Christian title), creatura a created thing (creature), re-creare to restore (recreation re-makes a person), pro-creare to beget, crescere to grow — a crescent is the growing moon (French croissant the crescent roll), in-crementum an increase (increment), de-crescere to shrink (decrease), con-crescere to grow together — to harden (concrete), ac-crescere to grow to (accrue), crew is accrue clipped — reinforcements, recruit recroistre, to grow again."],
  mem: ["memor(记得的)来自印欧语 *(s)mer-(记、关心),重叠式 me-mor;同根有希腊语 merimna(挂念)、martys(证人——\"记得的人\",martyr)、mermeros(操心的)、梵语 smarati(记得)、英语 mourn(挂念——哀悼)。罗马人 memini 我记得,memoria 记忆(memory 经法语),memorabilis 值得记的(memorable),memorandum 应记之事(1540 年代),com-memorare 一起记——纪念(commemorate),re-memorari 回想(remember 经法语 remembrer),memento 是命令式\"记住\"(memento mori 记住你会死),memoir 是法语 mémoire 的记录,immemorial 记不起的,memorial 纪念碑;mens(心智)是同根的另一个词(mental、mention、mind)。",
    "memor (mindful) comes from PIE *(s)mer- (to remember, care), reduplicated me-mor; cognates are Greek merimna (care), martys (a witness — one who remembers; martyr), Sanskrit smarati (remembers), English mourn (to be mindful — to grieve). Roman memini I remember, memoria memory (through French), memorabilis worth remembering (memorable), memorandum a thing to be remembered (1540s), com-memorare to remember together (commemorate), re-memorari to call to mind (remember through French remembrer), memento the imperative \"remember\" (memento mori), memoir the French mémoire record, immemorial beyond memory, memorial; mens (mind) is the same root's other word (mental, mention, mind)."],
  put: ["putare(修剪、清算、思考)来自 putus(干净的),印欧语 *pau-(打、切);同根有 pavire(捣实——pavement)、希腊语 paiein(打)。罗马农夫 putare 修剪葡萄藤——把多余的剪掉;算账也是\"剪去\"——清算(putare rationem),于是引申为思考:puto 我认为。com-putare 一起算(compute 1630 年代,computer 1640 年代指算数的人),dis-putare 分开算——辩论(dispute),re-putare 反复算——reputation 是被人反复估量的名声,am-putare 剪去(amputate),de-putare 指派(deputy 被派的人),im-putare 记在账上(impute),count 是 computare 经法语 conter——数,也讲(conte 故事、account 账目与叙述、recount),discount 折扣,contar 西班牙语数和讲,raccontare 意大利语讲述。",
    "putare (to prune, to reckon, to think) comes from putus (clean), PIE *pau- (to strike, cut); cognates are pavire (to beat down — pavement) and Greek paiein (to strike). A Roman farmer putare pruned the vine — cutting off the surplus; settling accounts was also cutting away (putare rationem), and so came thinking: puto, I reckon. com-putare to reckon together (compute 1630s; a computer was a person who computed, 1640s), dis-putare to reckon apart — dispute, re-putare to reckon over — a reputation is what people keep reckoning you at, am-putare to cut away (amputate), de-putare to assign (deputy), im-putare to enter on the account (impute), count is computare through French conter — to count and to tell (a conte, an account, recount), discount, Spanish contar counts and narrates, Italian raccontare tells."],
  simil: ["similis(相似的)来自印欧语 *sem-(一),与 simul(同时)、semel(一次)、singulus、希腊语 homos(同)、hama(一起)、英语 same、some 同根——\"像\"就是\"同一个\"。罗马人 similitudo 相似(similitude),as-simulare 使相似——同化(assimilate),simulare 装得像——假装(simulate;simulation 1950 年代计算机义),dis-simulare 掩饰(dissimulate),simul-taneus 同时的(simultaneous 1650 年代),simulacrum 影像,resemble 是 re-simulare 经法语 resembler,semblance 外表,ensemble 一起(in-simul),assemble 集合(ad-simul),facsimile 是 fac simile\"做成相似的\"(fax 1948 缩写),verisimilitude 逼真,similar 1610 年代经法语 similaire。",
    "similis (like) comes from PIE *sem- (one), kin to simul (at the same time), semel (once), singulus, Greek homos (same), hama (together), English same and some — to be like is to be one. Roman similitudo likeness (similitude), as-simulare to make like — assimilate, simulare to act like — pretend (simulate; the computing sense 1950s), dis-simulare to hide behind a likeness (dissimulate), simul-taneus at the same time (simultaneous 1650s), simulacrum an image, resemble re-simulare through French resembler, semblance, ensemble in-simul, together, assemble ad-simul, facsimile fac simile, \"make it alike\" (fax 1948), verisimilitude, similar 1610s through French similaire."],
  test: ["testis(证人)是 *tri-st-i-(\"作为第三者站着的人\")——两方争执时在场的第三人;与 tres、stare 同根。罗马法要求立遗嘱有七位 testes;testari 作证、立遗嘱(testament 遗嘱;《旧约》《新约》是 testamentum 译希伯来语的\"约\"),testimonium 证词(testimony),at-testari 证实(attest),con-testari 请证人开庭——争讼(contest),de-testari 请神作证诅咒——憎恶(detest),pro-testari 公开作证——声明、抗议(protest;Protestant 是 1529 年施派尔帝国议会上\"抗议\"的诸侯),in-testatus 无遗嘱的,testicle 是 testiculus\"小证人\"——男性身份的见证,testify,test(检验)是另一个词——testum 陶罐(炼金的坩埚)。",
    "testis (a witness) is *tri-st-i-, \"one who stands as the third\" — the third party present when two dispute; kin to tres and stare. Roman law wanted seven testes for a will; testari to witness, to make a will (testament; the Old and New Testaments are testamentum for the Hebrew covenant), testimonium testimony, at-testari to bear witness (attest), con-testari to call witnesses and open a case — to contest, de-testari to call the gods to witness a curse — to detest, pro-testari to declare publicly — protest (the Protestants protested at the Diet of Speyer in 1529), in-testatus without a will, testicle testiculus, a little witness — evidence of manhood, testify; test the trial is another word, testum, an earthen pot — the assayer's crucible."],
  tort: ["torquere(扭)的分词 tortus 是这一族的主干(见 torqueo):tortura 扭——刑讯(torture 1540 年代),tortus 扭曲的——tort 是法律的\"民事侵权\"(法语\"错\"),tortuosus 曲折(tortuous),tormentum 是绞绳的投石机、也是刑具(torment),ex-torquere 拧出来(extort),re-torquere 拧回去——反驳(retort),dis-torquere 扭歪(distort),con-torquere(contortion),torch 是 torca 扭成的麻束火把,torque 扭矩(1880 年代),torsion 扭力,torticollis 斜颈,tortilla 是西班牙语\"小饼\"(torta 与此无关),nasturtium 是\"拧鼻子\"的辣味植物,turtle 与 tortoise 是 tortuca(\"扭脚的\"?)经法语 tortue。",
    "The participle tortus of torquere (to twist) carries the family (see torqueo): tortura a twisting — torture (1540s), tortus twisted — a tort is the law's civil wrong (French for wrong), tortuosus winding (tortuous), tormentum the rope-twisted catapult and the instrument of torture (torment), ex-torquere to wring out (extort), re-torquere to twist back — retort, dis-torquere to twist awry (distort), con-torquere (contortion), torch a torca of twisted tow, torque the twisting force (1880s), torsion, torticollis a twisted neck, nasturtium the nose-twister, and tortoise and turtle are tortuca through French tortue."],
  stimul: ["stimulus(赶牛的刺棒)来自印欧语 *(s)teig-(刺),同根有 in-stigare(刺激——instigate)、di-stinguere(用刺做记号——区分,distinct)、ex-stinguere(戳灭火——extinguish)、希腊语 stizein(刺——stigma 是刺出的记号)、英语 stick、sting、德语 stechen。罗马人用 stimulus 赶牛;stimulare 刺激(stimulate 1610 年代;stimulus 生理学 1680 年代),in-stinctus 是\"被刺着往前\"——本能(instinct),stigma 奴隶身上刺的烙印——后指耻辱,stiletto 与此有关,ticket 是法语 étiquette(贴上的小条——etiquette 是贴在宫廷墙上的规矩),stitch、steak(戳在签子上的肉)是日耳曼亲戚。",
    "stimulus (an ox-goad) comes from PIE *(s)teig- (to prick), shared with in-stigare (to goad on — instigate), di-stinguere (to mark by pricking — distinguish, distinct), ex-stinguere (to prick out a fire — extinguish), Greek stizein (to prick — a stigma is a pricked mark), English stick and sting, German stechen. Romans drove oxen with the stimulus; stimulare to goad (stimulate 1610s; stimulus in physiology 1680s), in-stinctus \"pricked onward\" — instinct, stigma the brand pricked into a slave, later disgrace, ticket French étiquette (a little note stuck on — etiquette was the rules posted on the palace wall), stitch and steak (meat on a stick) are Germanic cousins."],
  long: ["longus(长)来自印欧语 *dlongʰos(长),同根有英语 long、德语 lang、哥特语 laggs、梵语 dīrgha、希腊语 dolikhos(长跑 dolichos)。罗马人 longus 是空间和时间的长;longitudo 长度——经度(longitude,托勒密的\"长\"方向),longaevus 长寿,pro-longare 延长(prolong),e-longare 拉长——法语 éloigner 使远,longinquus 遥远,oblong 长方形,lunge 是 allonger(伸长)经法语——击剑的刺,purloin 是 pro-longe\"拿远\"——偷,along 与此无关(英语),longevity 1610 年代,longshore 是 along shore。",
    "longus (long) comes from PIE *dlongʰos (long), shared with English long, German lang, Gothic laggs, Sanskrit dīrgha, Greek dolikhos (the long race). Roman longus was length in space and time; longitudo length — longitude (Ptolemy's \"long\" dimension), longaevus long-lived, pro-longare to extend (prolong), e-longare to lengthen away — French éloigner, to remove, longinquus distant, oblong, lunge allonger (to stretch out) through French — the fencer's thrust, purloin pro-longe, to take far off — to steal, longevity 1610s; along is native English."],
  arch: ["希腊语 arkhein(开始、领头、统治)与 arkhē(开端、统治权)词源不明。arkh-ōn 是雅典的执政官,arkh-angelos 首席天使,arkhi-tektōn 首席工匠(architect),arkh-iatros 御医(德语 Arzt 医生就是它!),arkhi-episkopos 大主教,mon-arkhia 一人统治,an-arkhia 无人统治,hier-arkhia 圣职等级,oligo-arkhia 少数统治,arkhaios 属于开端的——古老(archaic、archaeology 1600 年代),arkheion 官署——档案(archive),arkhetypos 原型(荣格 1919),arch- 前缀\"首要\"(archenemy、archbishop),-arch 后缀\"统治者\"(matriarch、patriarch、tetrarch),arkhi- 磨成 arci- 意大利语 arcivescovo,法语 archi-。",
    "Greek arkhein (to begin, lead, rule) and arkhē (beginning, sovereignty) are of unknown origin. An arkh-ōn was an Athenian magistrate, arkh-angelos the chief angel, arkhi-tektōn the master builder (architect), arkh-iatros the court physician (German Arzt, doctor, is this word!), arkhi-episkopos the archbishop, mon-arkhia rule by one, an-arkhia rule by none, hier-arkhia sacred ranking, olig-arkhia rule by few, arkhaios of the beginning — ancient (archaic, archaeology 1600s), arkheion the magistrates' office — archives, arkhetypos the original mould (Jung 1919), the prefix arch- chief (archenemy, archbishop), the suffix -arch a ruler (matriarch, patriarch, tetrarch), Italian arci- (arcivescovo), French archi-."],
  plac: ["placere(使喜悦、合意)与 placare(安抚)来自印欧语 *pleh₂k-(平、平静),同根有 planus(平)、希腊语 plax(平面)、英语 flat?存疑、德语 flach。罗马元老院的决议叫 placitum(\"合意的\");placidus 平静的(placid),com-placere 很合意(complacent 自满是\"很满意自己\"),dis-plicere 不合意(displease),placebo 是\"我将取悦\"——《诗篇》116 的晚祷首句,后指讨好的人,1785 年医学上指安慰剂,plea 是 placitum 经法语 plait——诉讼(plead、please 是 placere 经法语 plaisir),pleasant、pleasure 法语 plaisir,placate 安抚(1670 年代),implacable 不可安抚,plaisir 法语乐趣,piacere 意大利语(\"幸会\"),placer 西班牙语。",
    "placere (to please) and placare (to soothe) come from PIE *pleh₂k- (flat, calm), shared with planus (level), Greek plax (a flat surface), German flach. A Roman senate decision was a placitum, \"what has pleased\"; placidus calm (placid), com-placere to please greatly (complacent, pleased with oneself), dis-plicere to displease, placebo \"I shall please\" — the opening of vespers for the dead from Psalm 116, then a flatterer, and in 1785 a medicine given to please, plea placitum through French plait — a lawsuit (plead; please is placere through French plaisir), pleasant, pleasure, placate (1670s), implacable, French plaisir, Italian piacere (\"pleased to meet you\"), Spanish placer."],
  punct: ["pungere(刺)的分词 punctus 是这一族的根;印欧语 *peuǵ-(刺),同根有 pugnus(拳)、pugio(匕首)、希腊语 pygmē(拳——pygmy 是\"一肘高的人\")。punctum 是刺出的一点——点(point 经法语 point;英语 point 也混入了 puncta 尖端),punctuare 加点(punctuation 1530 年代),punctualis 到点的(punctual 1670 年代指准时),punctura 刺孔(puncture),ex-pungere 用点标出删除(expunge),com-pungere 刺痛良心(compunction),acu-puncture 针刺,pun-gent 刺鼻的,poignant 是 pungens 经法语——刺心的,pounce 是 punctiare 猛戳,appoint 是 ad-punctum 定点,disappoint,counterpoint 是 punctus contra punctum 点对点(音符),pink 剪花边是 pungere,punch 打孔机。",
    "The participle punctus of pungere (to prick) roots this family; PIE *peuǵ- (to prick), shared with pugnus (fist), pugio (dagger), Greek pygmē (a fist — a pygmy is a cubit tall). punctum is a pricked point — point through French (English point also absorbed puncta, a tip), punctuare to add points (punctuation 1530s), punctualis to the point (punctual meant on time by the 1670s), punctura a hole (puncture), ex-pungere to mark with dots for deletion (expunge), com-pungere to prick the conscience (compunction), acu-puncture, pun-gent sharp-smelling, poignant pungens through French — piercing, pounce punctiare, appoint ad-punctum, to fix a point, disappoint, counterpoint punctus contra punctum — note against note, pink (to cut a zigzag edge) pungere, punch the hole-maker."],
  rat: ["reri(认为、计算)的分词 ratus 与名词 ratio(计算、理由)来自印欧语 *h₂reh₁-(思量、安排),同根有英语 read(古义解释、猜)、riddle、德语 Rede(话)、raten(建议)、哥特语 rathjo(数)。罗马人 ratio 是账目、比率、也是理性(西塞罗译 logos);rationalis 理性的(rational),ratio 数学的比(ratio 1650 年代),ration 是法语的\"份\"(1550 年代),rationem reddere 交账,ratus 已定的——rate 经法语(先指估价),ratify 使定(ratificare),reason 是 ratio 经法语 raison,rational 与 rationale,irrational,ratiocination 推理,ratio 也在 pro rata 按比例,arraign 是 ad-rationare 传唤答辩。",
    "The participle ratus of reri (to reckon, judge) and the noun ratio (a reckoning, reason) come from PIE *h₂reh₁- (to think, arrange), shared with English read (once to interpret, guess), riddle, German Rede (speech), raten (to advise), Gothic rathjo (a number). Roman ratio was an account, a proportion and reason itself (Cicero's word for logos); rationalis rational, ratio the mathematical ratio (1650s), ration French for a share (1550s), rationem reddere to render account, ratus fixed — rate through French (first a valuation), ratify to make fixed, reason ratio through French raison, rational and rationale, irrational, ratiocination, pro rata, arraign ad-rationare, to call to account."],
  term: ["terminus(界石、终点)来自印欧语 *ter-(穿过、到达),同根有希腊语 terma(终点)、termōn(边界)、英语 through、thrill(穿透)、梵语 tarati(渡过)。罗马的界神 Terminus 受人祭拜(Terminalia,2 月 23 日),传说朱庇特神庙动工时他的界石拒绝挪动——罗马疆界永不后退;terminare 划界、结束(terminate),terminalis 末端的(terminal;1888 年铁路终点站),de-terminare 划定(determine;determination 先指划定后指决心),ex-terminare 赶出界外——消灭(exterminate),terminus 也是逻辑的\"项\"——term(1300 年代;学期 term 是固定的期限),terminology 1801,interminable 无尽,thrill 与 through 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "terminus (a boundary stone, an end) comes from PIE *ter- (to cross, reach), shared with Greek terma (goal), termōn (boundary), English through and thrill (to pierce), Sanskrit tarati (crosses). Rome worshipped the boundary god Terminus (the Terminalia, 23 February), and legend says his stone refused to move when the temple of Jupiter was built — Rome's borders would never recede; terminare to bound, to end (terminate), terminalis at the end (terminal; a railway terminal 1888), de-terminare to fix the limits (determine; determination meant fixing before resolve), ex-terminare to drive beyond the border — exterminate, terminus also the logician's term (1300s; a school term is a fixed period), terminology 1801, interminable; thrill and through are its Germanic cousins."],
  trib: ["tribus(部落)可能是 tri-(三)+ *bʰu-(存在)——传说罗马最初分三部落(Ramnes、Tities、Luceres);后来扩到 35 个 tribus,是投票单位。tribuere 是\"按部落分配\"——给予:tributum 是按部落征的税(tribute),tribunus 是部落的长官——保民官(tribune;论坛 tribune 是他讲话的高台),tribunal 是他的审判席,at-tribuere 分给——归因(attribute),con-tribuere 一起出(contribute),dis-tribuere 分发(distribute),re-tribuere 回报(retribution),tributary 纳贡的、支流(向大河\"纳贡\"),tribulation 与此无关(tribulum 打谷板——磨难)。",
    "tribus (a tribe) may be tri- (three) + *bʰu- (to be) — legend gave early Rome three tribes (Ramnes, Tities, Luceres); later there were 35, the units of voting. tribuere is to allot by tribe — to grant: tributum the tax levied by tribe (tribute), tribunus the tribe's officer — the tribune (a tribune is also the platform he spoke from), tribunal his bench, at-tribuere to assign to (attribute), con-tribuere to give together (contribute), dis-tribuere to deal out (distribute), re-tribuere to pay back (retribution), tributary paying tribute, and a river that \"pays\" a larger one; tribulation is unrelated (tribulum, a threshing-sledge)."],
  typ: ["希腊语 typos(打击、印痕、模子)来自 typtein(打),印欧语 *(s)teu-(打、推)。铸币的 typos 是模具压出的印记;罗马人借 typus 指图像、形式;神学的 typus 是《旧约》预表《新约》的\"型\"(typology),typographia 是 1550 年代给古腾堡技术起的名——\"用型印\",typewriter 1868,typical 1610 年代,typify,stereotype 是 1798 年的铅版印刷(1922 年李普曼转为\"刻板印象\"),archetype 原型,prototype 原型,type 在 1470 年代指象征,1710 年代指活字,daguerreotype 1839,tympanum(鼓——被打的)同根。",
    "Greek typos (a blow, an impression, a mould) comes from typtein (to strike), PIE *(s)teu- (to strike, push). A coin's typos was the die's impression; Rome borrowed typus for figure, form; the theologians' typus was an Old Testament figure foreshadowing the New (typology), typographia the 1550s name for Gutenberg's art — printing with types, typewriter 1868, typical 1610s, typify, stereotype the solid printing plate of 1798 (turned by Lippmann in 1922 into the fixed idea), archetype, prototype, type meaning a symbol in the 1470s and a piece of printing type in the 1710s, daguerreotype 1839; the tympanum, the beaten drum, is the same root."],
  fest: ["festus(节日的)与 feriae(假日)、fanum(神庙)来自印欧语 *dʰeh₁s-(神圣的事),与希腊语 theos 可能同根。罗马历上 dies festi 是敬神的日子;festum 节日(feast 经法语 feste,fête 法语),festivus 欢庆的(festive),festivitas(festivity),festivalis 1580 年代(festival),feria 是市集日——fair(经法语 feire:集市与节日同源),fanaticus 是\"神庙里的\"——被神附体的(fanatic;fan 是 1889 年美国棒球的缩写),profanus 神庙前的——世俗的(profane),fiesta 西班牙语,festa 意大利语,Oktoberfest 德语借词。",
    "festus (festal) with feriae (holidays) and fanum (a shrine) come from PIE *dʰeh₁s- (a sacred thing), perhaps kin to Greek theos. On the Roman calendar dies festi were days for the gods; festum a feast (through French feste; French fête), festivus joyful (festive), festivitas (festivity), festivalis 1580s (festival), feria the market day — fair (through French feire: fair and feast share a root), fanaticus \"of the temple\" — possessed (fanatic; fan is the 1889 American baseball clipping), profanus before the temple — worldly (profane), Spanish fiesta, Italian festa, Oktoberfest borrowed from German."],
  sort: ["sors(签、命运,属格 sortis)来自 serere(排列、串)——签是串在一起抽的;印欧语 *ser-(排)。罗马人抽签(sortitio)分配官职和行省;sors 是命运、也是一份(法语 sort 命运);sortiri 抽签——sortir 法语\"出去\"(从抽签的\"分得、脱出\"义演变),sortilegium 抽签占卜——巫术(sorcery 经法语 sorcerie),sorcier 巫师,con-sors 同命运的——共享者(consort 配偶),as-sortire 分类(assort),sort 从命运到种类(1380 年代)再到分类(1350 年代),sortie 出击,resort 是 re-sortir 再出去——依靠、去处,ensorcell 施魔法。",
    "sors (a lot, fate, genitive sortis) comes from serere (to arrange, string) — lots were strung together to be drawn; PIE *ser- (to line up). Romans drew lots (sortitio) for offices and provinces; sors was fate and a share (French sort, fate); sortiri to draw lots — French sortir \"to go out\" grew from \"to get by lot, to come out\", sortilegium lot-reading — witchcraft (sorcery through French sorcerie), sorcier a sorcerer, con-sors sharing one fate — a partner (consort), as-sortire to arrange by kind (assort), sort from fate to kind (1380s) and to sorting, sortie a going out, resort re-sortir, to go out again — a recourse, a place gone to, ensorcell to bewitch."],
  mont: ["mons(山,属格 montis)来自印欧语 *men-(突出),与 minere(突出)、mentum(下巴)同根。罗马建在七座 montes 上;montanus 山的(montane、Montana),mont-icellus 小山(Monticello),pro-montorium 岬角(promontory),trans-montanus 山那边的(意大利语 tramontana 北风——阿尔卑斯那边来的),ultra-montane 阿尔卑斯以北的(教皇派),mountain 是 montana 经法语 montaigne,mount 山、也是 montare 上去(mount、amount 是 ad montem——上山、达到数量,dismount、surmount 越过山,paramount 是 par amont\"在上面\"),Montmartre 殉道者山,Piedmont 山脚,Vermont 绿山,tantamount 是 tant amonter\"抵得上\"。",
    "mons (a mountain, genitive montis) comes from PIE *men- (to project), kin to minere (to jut) and mentum (chin). Rome stood on seven montes; montanus of mountains (montane, Montana), mont-icellus a little hill (Monticello), pro-montorium a headland (promontory), trans-montanus from beyond the mountains (Italian tramontana, the north wind over the Alps), ultra-montane beyond the Alps — papal, mountain montana through French montaigne, mount the hill and montare to climb (mount; amount is ad montem, uphill — to add up, dismount, surmount to climb over, paramount par amont, above), Montmartre the martyrs' mount, Piedmont the mountain's foot, Vermont green mountain, tantamount tant amonter, amounting to as much."],
  mond: ["mundus(世界、装饰、洁净)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——古人说它译自希腊语 kosmos(秩序、装饰、宇宙),所以 mundus 既是\"世界\"也是\"化妆品\"(mundus muliebris 女人的梳妆用品)。mundanus 世界的——世俗的(mundane 1400 年代,\"乏味\"义 19 世纪),mundialis 世界的(西班牙语 mundial 世界杯),monde 法语世界(tout le monde 所有人,beau monde 上流社会,demimonde 半上流),mondo 意大利语,mundo 西班牙语,im-mundus 不洁(immund 罕用),e-mundare 清洁,Mondrian 与此无关,mundify 净化。",
    "mundus (the world, adornment, cleanliness) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — the ancients said it translated Greek kosmos (order, ornament, universe), which is why mundus meant both the world and a woman's toilette (mundus muliebris). mundanus of the world — worldly (mundane 1400s; the sense of dull 19th century), mundialis worldwide (Spanish mundial, the World Cup), French monde (tout le monde everyone, beau monde high society, demimonde its shady half), Italian mondo, Spanish mundo, im-mundus unclean, e-mundare to clean, mundify to purify."],
  ciel: ["caelum(天)可能来自印欧语 *kai-lo-(整、完好——晴朗的天),与 caelare(雕刻)无关;同根的可能有英语 whole、hale、德语 heil。罗马人的 caelum 是天空也是天堂(基督教用它译希腊语 ouranos);caelestis 天上的(celestial),caeruleus 天蓝的(cerulean),ciel 法语天(ciel de lit 床顶、arc-en-ciel 彩虹),cielo 意大利语和西班牙语,ceiling 是中古英语 celing——可能受 caelum 影响的\"顶\",celeste 天蓝,Céline 是 Caelina 天的。",
    "caelum (sky, heaven) may come from PIE *kai-lo- (whole, unbroken — a clear sky), unrelated to caelare (to engrave); English whole and hale, German heil are possible cousins. Roman caelum was the sky and heaven (the Church used it for Greek ouranos); caelestis heavenly (celestial), caeruleus sky-blue (cerulean), French ciel (ciel de lit a bed-canopy, arc-en-ciel a rainbow), Italian and Spanish cielo, ceiling Middle English celing — a \"heaven\" overhead, perhaps shaped by caelum, celeste sky-blue, Céline from Caelina, of the sky."],
  rest: ["restare(留下、剩下)是 re-(回)+ stare(站):站在后面不走的。罗马人 restat 是\"还剩下\";restare 经法语 rester 成了\"停留、剩下\"(rest 剩余义与 rest 休息是两个词——休息是古英语 ræst),restaurant 是 restaurare(恢复)的法语分词——1765 年巴黎布朗热的\"补身汤\"店首次以此为名,restore 恢复(re-staurare,staurare 与 stare 同根——竖立),restoration 王政复辟(1660),arrest 是 ad-restare 使停住(1300 年代),rester 法语,restar 西班牙语减去,restare 意大利语,restive 本义\"停着不动\"——后转为躁动。",
    "restare (to remain) is re- (back) + stare (to stand): what stands back and stays. Roman restat is \"there remains\"; restare through French rester became to stay, to be left (rest the remainder and rest as repose are different words — the latter Old English ræst), restaurant is the French participle of restaurare (to restore) — Boulanger's Paris shop of 1765 first sold \"restorative\" broths under that name, restore re-staurare (staurare kin to stare — to set upright), the Restoration of 1660, arrest ad-restare, to bring to a stop (1300s), French rester, Spanish restar to subtract, Italian restare, restive meant standing still before it meant fidgety."],
  guerr: ["法语 guerre、意大利语 guerra、西班牙语 guerra(战争)不是拉丁语 bellum 的后代,而是法兰克语 *werra(混乱、争斗)——与英语 war(古英语从诺曼法语 werre 借)、德语 verwirren(搅乱)、wirr(混乱)同根,印欧语 *wers-(搅混)。罗马人的 bellum 因与 bellus(美)同形而在口语里被弃用,日耳曼雇佣兵的词填了空;guerrilla 是西班牙语\"小战争\"(1808 年半岛战争,英国借入),guerrier 法语战士,warrior 是诺曼法语 werreier,guerre 也给了 guerdon?否(那是 widar-lon 回报)。",
    "French guerre, Italian and Spanish guerra (war) do not descend from Latin bellum but from Frankish *werra (confusion, strife) — kin to English war (Old English took werre from Norman French), German verwirren (to confuse), wirr (tangled), PIE *wers- (to mix up). Roman bellum, sounding like bellus (pretty), fell out of everyday use, and the word of the Germanic soldiery filled the gap; guerrilla is Spanish \"little war\" (the Peninsular War of 1808, borrowed into English), guerrier the French fighter, warrior Norman French werreier."],
  casa: ["casa(小屋、茅舍)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能是外来的乡村词。古典拉丁语的房子是 domus,casa 只是农民的棚屋;但在晚期口语里 casa 取代了 domus,所以西班牙语、意大利语、葡萄牙语的\"家\"都是 casa(法语用了 mansio——maison)。casula 小屋——教士的十字褡 chasuble(像小屋一样罩着),casino 是意大利语\"小房子\"——乡间别墅、社交俱乐部,1850 年代才指赌场,cassock 可能经法语 casaque,caserne 兵营是普罗旺斯语 cazerna,casement 与此无关。",
    "casa (a hut, a cottage) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin — probably a rustic loan. The classical house was the domus, and casa only a peasant's shack; but late speech let casa replace domus, so home in Spanish, Italian and Portuguese is casa (French took mansio — maison). casula a little hut — the priest's chasuble that covers like one, casino Italian \"little house\" — a country lodge, a club, and only in the 1850s a gambling house, cassock perhaps through French casaque, caserne barracks from Provençal cazerna; casement is unrelated."],
  hemi: ["希腊语 hēmi-(半)来自印欧语 *sēmi-(半),与拉丁语 semi- 同根(希腊语 s- 变 h-);德语没有亲戚。hēmi-sphairion 半球(hemisphere 1380 年代),hēmi-kranion 半头痛——migraine 是它经晚期拉丁语 hemicrania 和法语 migraine 的磨损,hemi-cycle 半圆会场,hemi-plegia 半身不遂,hemi-stich 半行诗;拉丁语 semi-:semi-circle、semi-final、semi-colon(1640 年代)、semester 是 sex-mensis 六个月(德语 Semester 借入英语),sesqui-(一倍半)是 semis-que。",
    "Greek hēmi- (half) comes from PIE *sēmi- (half), kin to Latin semi- (Greek s- became h-). hēmi-sphairion a half-globe (hemisphere 1380s), hēmi-kranion a pain in half the head — migraine is its worn form through late Latin hemicrania and French migraine, hemi-cycle, hemi-plegia paralysis of one side, hemi-stich a half-line; Latin semi-: semi-circle, semi-final, semi-colon (1640s), semester sex-mensis, six months (via German Semester), sesqui- (one and a half) semis-que."],
  hexa: ["希腊语 hex(六)是印欧语 *sweḱs:拉丁语 sex、梵语 ṣaṭ、英语 six、德语 sechs(希腊语 s- 变 h-)。hexa-gōnon 六角形(hexagon 1560 年代——法国人自称 l'Hexagone),hexa-metros 六音步(荷马与维吉尔的史诗格律),hexa-hedron 六面体,hexa-decimal 十六进制(1950 年代),Hexateuch 六经;拉丁语 sex-:sextant 六分仪(1620 年代),sextet,sexagenarian 六十岁的,siesta 是 sexta hora 第六小时——正午小睡(西班牙语),sext 午课经。",
    "Greek hex (six) is Indo-European *sweḱs: Latin sex, Sanskrit ṣaṭ, English six, German sechs (Greek s- became h-). hexa-gōnon six-angled (hexagon 1560s — the French call their country l'Hexagone), hexa-metros the six-foot line of Homer and Virgil, hexa-hedron, hexa-decimal (1950s), the Hexateuch; Latin sex-: sextant (1620s), sextet, sexagenarian, siesta sexta hora, the sixth hour — the midday nap (Spanish), sext the noon office."],
  encephal: ["希腊语 en-kephalos 是\"头里面的\"——脑(kephalē 头,与拉丁语 caput、英语 head 同根 *kaput-);亚里士多德以为 enkephalos 只是冷却血液的器官。encephalitis 脑炎(1840 年代;1917 年昏睡性脑炎大流行),encephalon 脑(解剖学),electro-encephalo-gram 脑电图(贝格尔 1929),encephalopathy 脑病,hydro-cephalus 脑积水,cephalo-pod 头足类(章鱼),Kephalonia 岛,cephalic 头的,acephalous 无头的,brachy-cephalic 短头的(19 世纪人类学)。",
    "Greek en-kephalos is what lies within the head — the brain (kephalē, head, shares *kaput- with Latin caput and English head); Aristotle thought the enkephalos merely cooled the blood. encephalitis (1840s; the sleeping-sickness pandemic of 1917), encephalon the brain in anatomy, electro-encephalo-gram (Berger 1929), encephalopathy, hydro-cephalus water on the brain, cephalo-pod head-footed (the octopus), Kephalonia, cephalic, acephalous headless, brachy-cephalic short-headed (19th-century anthropology)."],
  olig: ["希腊语 oligos(少)词源不明,可能与 loigos(毁灭)有关。olig-archia 少数人统治——亚里士多德的坏政体之一(oligarchy 1570 年代;1990 年代用于俄罗斯寡头),olig-o-poly 少数卖家(1895 年仿 monopoly),oligo-cene 渐新世(1854,\"少新的\"),oligo-phrenia 智力低下,oligarch 1610 年代,oligo-saccharide 寡糖,oligo-nucleotide。",
    "Greek oligos (few) is of uncertain origin, perhaps linked to loigos (ruin). olig-archia rule by the few — one of Aristotle's corrupt constitutions (oligarchy 1570s; applied to Russia's tycoons in the 1990s), olig-o-poly few sellers (1895, on the model of monopoly), the Oligo-cene epoch (1854, \"few new\" species), oligo-phrenia, oligarch 1610s, oligo-saccharide, oligo-nucleotide."],
  mnem: ["希腊语 mnēmē(记忆)来自 mimnēskesthai(记得),印欧语 *men-(想),与拉丁语 mens、memini、英语 mind 同根。Mnēmosynē(记忆女神)是九缪斯之母——诗人靠记忆歌唱;mnēmonikos 助记的(mnemonic 1750 年代),a-mnēsia 失忆(1786),a-mnēstia 是\"遗忘\"——前 403 年雅典民主恢复后对内战的\"不追究\"(amnesty 1570 年代),ana-mnēsis 回忆(柏拉图的学习即回忆;医学的病史),hyper-mnesia 记忆过强,mnemotechnics 记忆术。",
    "Greek mnēmē (memory) comes from mimnēskesthai (to remember), PIE *men- (to think), kin to Latin mens and memini and English mind. Mnēmosynē, Memory, was the mother of the nine Muses — the poet sang from memory; mnēmonikos aiding memory (mnemonic 1750s), a-mnēsia loss of memory (1786), a-mnēstia a forgetting — the \"no recrimination\" decree when Athenian democracy was restored in 403 BC (amnesty 1570s), ana-mnēsis recollection (Plato's learning as remembering; a doctor's case history), hyper-mnesia, mnemotechnics."],
  arthr: ["希腊语 arthron(关节)来自 ararein(接合),印欧语 *h₂er-(装配),与 harmonia、拉丁语 ars、arma、articulus(小关节——article 条款、articulate 分节清楚)同根。希波克拉底文集有 arthritis;arthro-poda 节肢动物(1845,\"关节的脚\"),arthro-scopy 1918,arthro-plasty 关节成形,arthralgia 关节痛,arthrosis,article 是拉丁线的 articulus——关节、条目,articulate 有关节的——发音清楚,arthritic 1370 年代。",
    "Greek arthron (a joint) comes from ararein (to fit together), PIE *h₂er- (to fit), kin to harmonia, Latin ars, arma and articulus (a little joint — an article, a clause; articulate, jointed). The Hippocratic corpus has arthritis; arthro-poda jointed-foot animals (1845), arthro-scopy 1918, arthro-plasty, arthralgia, arthrosis; article is the Latin line's articulus — a joint, an item, articulate jointed — clearly spoken, arthritic 1370s."],
  my: ["希腊语 mys(肌肉、老鼠,属格 myos)与拉丁语 mus、英语 mouse 同根(印欧语 *mūs)——肌肉像皮下跑动的小老鼠(拉丁语 musculus\"小老鼠\"就是 muscle)。myo-kardion 心肌(1880 年代),my-algia 肌痛,myo-pathy 肌病,myo-globin 肌红蛋白(1930 年代),myo-sin 肌球蛋白(1864),myo-sitis 肌炎,myasthenia 肌无力,electro-myo-graphy 肌电图;拉丁语 musculus 还给了 mussel(贻贝,像小老鼠)。",
    "Greek mys (a muscle, a mouse, genitive myos) shares PIE *mūs with Latin mus and English mouse — a muscle looks like a little mouse running under the skin (Latin musculus, \"little mouse\", is muscle). myo-kardion the heart muscle (1880s), my-algia muscle pain, myo-pathy, myo-globin (1930s), myo-sin (1864), myo-sitis, myasthenia muscle weakness, electro-myo-graphy; Latin musculus also gave mussel, the mouse-like shellfish."],
  latr: ["希腊语 latreia(雇佣劳动、侍奉、崇拜)来自 latris(雇工),词源不明。《七十士译本》用 latreuein 指侍奉神;教父区分 latreia(只给上帝的崇拜)与 doulia(给圣人的敬礼);eidōlo-latreia 拜偶像(idolatry 1200 年代经法语),idolater,Mario-latry 是新教对天主教敬礼圣母的贬称(1610 年代),biblio-latry 拜圣经,-latry 后缀 19 世纪能产(demonolatry、zoolatry)。",
    "Greek latreia (hired service, worship) comes from latris (a hired servant), of unknown origin. The Septuagint used latreuein for serving God; the Fathers distinguished latreia (adoration owed to God alone) from doulia (veneration of the saints); eidōlo-latreia image-worship (idolatry 1200s via French), idolater, Mario-latry the Protestant jibe at Catholic devotion to Mary (1610s), biblio-latry, and -latry a productive 19th-century suffix (demonolatry, zoolatry)."],
  acou: ["希腊语 akouein(听)来自印欧语 *h₂keh₂ous-(锐耳),同根有英语 hear、德语 hören、哥特语 hausjan。akoustikos 关于听的——acoustic 是 1600 年代培根从希腊语借的\"听觉的\"(acoustics 1683),acoumeter 听力计,hyper-acusis 听觉过敏,presby-acusis 老年性耳聋,acousma?,akousmatikoi 是毕达哥拉斯的\"听讲者\";hear 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "Greek akouein (to hear) comes from PIE *h₂keh₂ous- (sharp-eared), shared with English hear, German hören, Gothic hausjan. akoustikos of hearing — acoustic was Bacon's 1600s borrowing (acoustics 1683), acoumeter a hearing-gauge, hyper-acusis over-sensitive hearing, presby-acusis old-age deafness, the akousmatikoi Pythagoras' listeners; hear is its Germanic cousin."],
  rhet: ["希腊语 rhētōr(演说家)来自 eirein(说,词干 rhe-/wer-),印欧语 *werh₁-(说),与拉丁语 verbum、英语 word 同根。雅典的 rhētōr 是在公民大会发言的政治家;rhētorikē (technē) 是柏拉图《高尔吉亚》里的\"说话术\"(rhetoric 1300 年代经拉丁语 rhetorica——中世纪三艺之一),rhetorical 1470 年代,rhetorician,rhetorical question 1500 年代,rhematic 与 rheme(述题)是 rhēma\"说出的东西\"——动词。",
    "Greek rhētōr (a speaker) comes from eirein (to say, stem rhe-/wer-), PIE *werh₁- (to speak), kin to Latin verbum and English word. The Athenian rhētōr was the politician who addressed the assembly; rhētorikē (technē) is the art of speaking of Plato's Gorgias (rhetoric 1300s via Latin rhetorica — one of the medieval trivium), rhetorical 1470s, rhetorician, the rhetorical question 1500s, and rheme (the predicate) is rhēma, a thing said — the verb."],
  melo: ["希腊语 melos(肢体、乐句、歌)词源不明——一句旋律是歌的\"肢体\"。mel-ōidia 歌的调子(melody 1300 年代经法语),melo-drama 是 18 世纪法语 mélodrame——配音乐的戏,melodic 1820 年代,melodious,melisma 是\"歌\"——一音多字,Melpomene 是\"歌唱者\"缪斯(悲剧),melic 抒情诗,melodica 1958 霍纳的口风琴。",
    "Greek melos (a limb, a phrase of music, a song) is of unknown origin — a strain of melody is the song's limb. mel-ōidia the song's tune (melody 1300s via French), melo-drama 18th-century French mélodrame, a play with music, melodic 1820s, melodious, melisma \"a song\" — many notes to one syllable, Melpomene the singing Muse (of tragedy), melic lyric verse, the melodica Hohner's 1958 instrument."],
  aristo: ["希腊语 aristos(最好的)是 agathos(好)的最高级,来自 *ar-(合适,与 harmonia、ars 同根)。aristo-kratia 是\"最优者统治\"——柏拉图、亚里士多德理想中由德才兼备者执政的政体,后指世袭贵族(aristocracy 1560 年代),aristocrat 是 1789 年法国大革命的敌人称呼,Aristotle 是\"最好的目的\",Aristophanes\"最好的显现\",Aristides\"最好的儿子\",aristology 是 1835 年的\"饮食学\"(戏谑)。",
    "Greek aristos (best) is the superlative of agathos (good), from *ar- (fitting, kin to harmonia and ars). aristo-kratia is rule by the best — the constitution Plato and Aristotle imagined for men of virtue and ability, later the hereditary nobility (aristocracy 1560s), aristocrat the enemy named by the French Revolution of 1789, Aristotle \"best purpose\", Aristophanes \"best appearing\", Aristides \"best son\", aristology the 1835 jocular science of dining."],
  steno: ["希腊语 stenos(窄)词源不明。steno-graphia 是 1602 年威利斯给速记起的名——\"窄写\"(stenography),stenographer 1795,steno 1906 美国缩写,steno-sis 狭窄(1860 年代医学),steno-thermal 窄温的,steno-haline,stenotype 速记机(1880 年代)。",
    "Greek stenos (narrow) is of unknown origin. steno-graphia was Willis' 1602 name for shorthand — narrow writing (stenography), stenographer 1795, steno the 1906 American clipping, steno-sis a narrowing (medical 1860s), steno-thermal tolerating a narrow temperature range, steno-haline, the stenotype machine (1880s)."],
  cryo: ["希腊语 kryos(霜、冰冷)来自印欧语 *kreus-(冻结、结壳),同根有拉丁语 crusta(壳——crust、custard)、crystallum(冰——水晶 crystal,希腊人以为水晶是永冻的冰)、英语 无。cryo- 全是现代词:cryo-genic 1896(产生低温的),cryo-surgery 1961,cryo-therapy 1900 年代,cryo-preservation 1960 年代,cryonics 1965(仿 bionics),crystal 是它最古老的后代,krystallos 也指玻璃透镜。",
    "Greek kryos (frost, icy cold) comes from PIE *kreus- (to freeze, form a crust), shared with Latin crusta (a crust — crust, custard) and crystallum (ice — crystal; the Greeks thought rock crystal was ice frozen for ever). cryo- is all modern: cryo-genic 1896, cryo-surgery 1961, cryo-therapy 1900s, cryo-preservation 1960s, cryonics 1965 (on bionics); crystal is its oldest descendant, and krystallos also meant a glass lens."],
  martyr: ["希腊语 martys(证人,属格 martyros)来自印欧语 *(s)mer-(记得),与拉丁语 memor、英语 mourn 同根——证人是\"记得的人\"。《新约》的 martys 是为基督作见证的人;2 世纪起专指以死作证的人(《波利卡普殉道记》约 155 年),拉丁语 martyr,古英语 martyr 是最早的教会借词之一;martyrium 殉道者纪念堂,martyrology 殉道者名录,martyrdom 1200 年代,martyr 作动词\"折磨\"1300 年代,martyr complex 1920 年代。",
    "Greek martys (a witness, genitive martyros) comes from PIE *(s)mer- (to remember), kin to Latin memor and English mourn — a witness is one who remembers. The New Testament's martys bears witness to Christ; from the 2nd century it meant one who witnessed by dying (the Martyrdom of Polycarp, c. 155), Latin martyr, Old English martyr among the earliest church loans; a martyrium a martyr's shrine, martyrology the roll of martyrs, martyrdom 1200s, martyr as a verb to torment 1300s, martyr complex 1920s."],
  apocal: ["希腊语 apo-kalyptein 是\"揭开盖子\"(kalyptein 遮,与拉丁语 celare、英语 hell 同根 *ḱel-)。apokalypsis 是揭示——《约翰启示录》的第一个词(Apokalypsis Iēsou Christou),拉丁语译 Revelatio;apocalypse 在 1200 年代指这卷书,1800 年代才指世界末日(书中描写的);apocalyptic 1660 年代,Calypso 是\"隐藏者\"——把奥德修斯藏了七年的女神,eucalyptus 是 1788 年的\"好好盖着的\"(花蕾有盖),calyx 花萼(盖花的)。",
    "Greek apo-kalyptein is to take the cover off (kalyptein to hide, PIE *ḱel-, kin to Latin celare and English hell). apokalypsis is an uncovering — the first word of John's Revelation (Apokalypsis Iēsou Christou), Latin Revelatio; apocalypse named the book in the 1200s and the end of the world it describes only in the 1800s; apocalyptic 1660s, Calypso the hider — the goddess who kept Odysseus seven years, eucalyptus the 1788 \"well-covered\" (its buds have lids), calyx the flower's cover."],
  sthen: ["希腊语 sthenos(力量)词源不明。a-sthenia 无力(asthenia 1800 年代医学),neur-asthenia 是比尔德 1869 年的\"神经衰弱\"——19 世纪末美国的时代病,calli-sthenics 是 1827 年的\"美与力\"体操,Sthenno 是三戈耳工之一(\"强大的\"),Demosthenes 是\"人民的力量\",asthenosphere 软流圈(1914),hypersthenia 亢奋。",
    "Greek sthenos (strength) is of unknown origin. a-sthenia weakness (medical 1800s), neur-asthenia Beard's 1869 nerve-weakness — the fashionable ailment of late-19th-century America, calli-sthenics the 1827 exercises of beauty and strength, Sthenno one of the three Gorgons (\"the mighty\"), Demosthenes \"strength of the people\", the asthenosphere (1914), hypersthenia."],
  hipp: ["希腊语 hippos(马)是印欧语 *h₁eḱwos:拉丁语 equus、梵语 aśva、古英语 eoh(马)、高卢语 epos(Epona 马神)。hippo-dromos 赛马场,hippo-potamos 河马——\"河里的马\"(希罗多德),Hippo-kratēs 希波克拉底\"马的力量\",Philippos\"爱马者\"(腓力),hippo-kampos 海马——脑里的海马体因形似得名(1580 年代解剖学),hippo-logy 马学,hippie 与此无关,Xanthippe\"黄马\"(苏格拉底之妻),eohippus 是 1876 年的\"黎明马\"化石。",
    "Greek hippos (horse) is Indo-European *h₁eḱwos: Latin equus, Sanskrit aśva, Old English eoh, Gaulish epos (the horse-goddess Epona). hippo-dromos a racecourse, hippo-potamos the river-horse (Herodotus), Hippo-kratēs \"horse-power\", Philippos lover of horses (Philip), hippo-kampos the sea-horse — the brain's hippocampus named for its shape (1580s anatomy), hippo-logy, Xanthippe \"yellow horse\" (Socrates' wife), eohippus the 1876 dawn-horse fossil."],
  ornith: ["希腊语 ornis(鸟,属格 ornithos)来自印欧语 *h₃er-(大鸟、鹰),同根有英语 erne(白尾海雕)、德语 Aar、赫梯语 haras、古教会斯拉夫语 orĭlŭ(鹰)。亚里士多德的《动物志》已系统分类鸟;ornitho-logia 1670 年代(雷与威卢比的《鸟类学》1676),ornithologist 1670 年代,ornitho-rhynchus 鸭嘴兽\"鸟嘴\"(1800),ornitho-pter 扑翼机,Ornithomimus 1890\"鸟模仿者\"恐龙,ornithosis 鸟疫。",
    "Greek ornis (a bird, genitive ornithos) comes from PIE *h₃er- (large bird, eagle), shared with English erne (the sea-eagle), German Aar, Hittite haras, Old Church Slavonic orĭlŭ (eagle). Aristotle's History of Animals already classified birds; ornitho-logia 1670s (Ray and Willughby's Ornithologia of 1676), ornithologist 1670s, ornitho-rhynchus the bird-billed platypus (1800), ornitho-pter a flapping-wing aircraft, Ornithomimus the 1890 bird-mimic dinosaur, ornithosis."],
  entom: ["希腊语 entomon(昆虫)是 en-temnein\"切进去的\"——身体分节;亚里士多德的 ta entoma 就是\"分节的动物\",拉丁语 insectum 是它的直译(in-secare)。entomo-logia 1760 年代法语(entomology 1766),entomologist 1770 年代,entomo-phagy 食虫,entomo-philous 虫媒的;-tomy(切)同根。",
    "Greek entomon (an insect) is en-temnein, \"cut into\" — the body's segments; Aristotle's ta entoma are the notched animals, and Latin insectum (in-secare) is a literal translation. entomo-logia 1760s French (entomology 1766), entomologist 1770s, entomo-phagy insect-eating, entomo-philous insect-pollinated; -tomy (cutting) is the same root."],
});
